From 126baaca4fd430c8da3388b2c1f45ec74d2bce7e Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Romain Dolbeau <romain@dolbeau.org>
Date: Sun, 9 Aug 2026 08:29:08 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 1/5] hw/scsi: a software NCR 5380

QEMU has no NCR 5380.  grep over the tree finds a Linux bootinfo tag and
a line of captured `ls` output used as QDict test data, and nothing else;
hw/m68k/q800.c models a Macintosh that really had one and uses an ESP
instead.  This adds the part, as a chip separate from any board that
carries it - the split esp.c makes between TYPE_ESP and the three boards
around it, and for the same reason: every 5380 machine presented the same
eight registers, because the part decoded /CS with A0..A2 on the die
(design manual p. 6), and differed only in where they landed and in what
answered DRQ.

It models wires and not commands, which is the one design decision worth
arguing.  A 5380 has no sequencer: section 7 of the manual (p. 18) lists
the whole of what is in hardware - DMA transfers, arbitration, phase
monitoring, disconnection, bus reset, parity, selection - and no part of
a command is on that list.  Every register a driver writes is a pad, and
Current SCSI Bus Status is a window straight onto the bus.  esp.c can be
command-level because an ESP really is a sequencer and the guest kicks
it with one byte; a 5380 written that way would have to invent a hidden
phase machine and then re-derive the wires from it to answer three of
its own registers.  So the chip drives wires, and an adapter at the
bottom of the file turns bus phases into scsi_req_new, scsi_req_continue
and complete.  That adapter is the only part that knows what a CDB is.

There is no time in the model.  The two delays the part implements - bus
free and bus settle, both 400 ns - exist to filter a real cable, and a
software fabric has no glitches to filter, so both collapse to "BSY is
false once the wires have settled".  What is deliberately *not* here is
the 2.2 us arbitration delay, which the manual says the chip does not
implement and "must be implemented in the controlling software driver"
(p. 18); nor the selection timeout, which is the driver's for the same
reason - a target that is not there simply never asserts BSY.  Putting
either in would make a driver that omitted its half work when against
real silicon it would not.

Wires settling is the part that has to be iterated by hand, and it is
subtler than it looks.  What this chip drives depends on what it sees:
as an initiator it drives the data lines only while the phase matches,
and the phase is on wires it may itself have been driving a moment ago.
Leaving target mode is the case that bites.  The chip stops driving MSG
and C/D, the phase it was matching disappears, and it must stop driving
the data bus in the same instant - so a single pass leaves the old byte
sitting on the bus until something else happens to disturb it.  The
combinational half therefore runs to its own fixed point before each
edge, and the wires are part of the loop's termination test as well as
the state.  Hardware gets this for nothing, being a comb block; in C it
is a loop, and the loop has to be told what it is converging on.

BUSY ERROR is the same shape seen from the other side.  It is a level -
"set whenever the MONITOR BUSY bit is true and BSY is false" (p. 16) -
so the condition that filled the latch is still true after a driver has
read register 7, and the model has to ask for the pass that fills it
again rather than treating the read as the end of it.  A latch that
emptied and stayed empty would make NCR5380_intr's careful ordering,
Mode Register before register 7, pointless, and would let a driver that
got it backwards appear to work.

Interrupts are the six the manual lists.  Arbitration reports AIP and
LOST ARBITRATION and nothing else, because the chip does not decide who
won - comparing IDs against the data bus is the driver's job.

Disconnection, reselection, tagged queueing, linked commands and
checking incoming parity are all absent on purpose.  A model that
quietly acquired one of them would let a driver missing its half appear
to work.

Both of the paragraphs above about settling are here because the first
draft got them wrong, and what caught them was a differential harness:
this model and an independent SystemVerilog one driven through identical
register sequences with every read compared.  They now agree over
thirteen scripted sequences, one per datasheet trap, and over eight
seeds of a twenty-thousand-step random walk - about 68000 reads.
